  <title>Unprocessed orders</title>

  <p>When the vendor's invoice arrives for a <link
  xref="purchase_order">purchase order</link>, check it against the order
  here and save it as a bill to pay. Open
  <guiseq><gui>Vendor</gui><gui>Unprocessed orders</gui></guiseq>, or the
  <gui>Unprocessed Orders</gui> button of the main window. Every posted
  purchase order without an invoice is listed.</p>

  <steps>
    <item><p>Choose the <gui>Purchase Order</gui>.</p></item>
    <item><p>Type the vendor's <gui>Invoice number</gui>, or another
    description of their invoice.</p></item>
    <item><p>Change each line's <gui>Qty</gui> and <gui>Price</gui> to what
    the vendor actually invoiced. A sum such as <input>2x12</input> works in
    <gui>Qty</gui>; see <link xref="qty_arithmetic"/>.</p></item>
    <item><p>Choose an <gui>Expense Account</gui> for any line that has none.
    Until every line has one, the save button reads <gui>Missing expense
    account</gui>.</p></item>
    <item><p>Add freight and other charges, as below.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>Save as invoice</gui>. The bill now waits in <link
    xref="vendor_payment"/>, and the order in <link
    xref="receive_orders"/>.</p></item>
  </steps>

  <p>To pay the bill straight away, click <gui>Save and pay ...</gui>
  instead. It saves the invoice just as <gui>Save as invoice</gui> does,
  asking first about a missing attachment, then opens <link
  xref="vendor_payment"/> with this vendor and bill already chosen.</p>

  <section id="attachment">
  <title>The vendor's invoice as a file</title>
  <p><gui>Attach...</gui> keeps a scan or PDF of the vendor's invoice with the
  order; see <link xref="pdf_attachment"/>. <gui>View attachment</gui> opens
  it. If <link xref="settings">the settings</link> ask for an attachment,
  saving without one asks whether to attach it first or go on without
  it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="expenses">
  <title>Freight and other charges</title>
  <p>Choose an expense product, such as freight, under the lines, type its
  amount, and click <gui>Add to Invoice</gui>. <gui>Expense products</gui>
  opens the list of expense products to add one; see <link
  xref="expense_products"/>.</p>
  <p><gui>Calculate cost</gui> spreads the expense lines evenly over every
  unit ordered, into <gui>Calculated cost</gui>.
  Or type a <gui>Surcharge</gui> on a line, as an amount per unit or a
  percent such as <input>5%</input>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost">
  <title>Updating product costs</title>
  <p>When a line's cost is higher than the product's current cost, or lower
  by more than the cost decrease alert in <link xref="settings"/>, the
  <gui>Cost update</gui> window asks whether to update the product's cost.
  It also shows each price term's <gui>Markup %</gui> and <gui>Selling
  price</gui>; change either one and the other follows. Click
  <gui>Update</gui> to save them, or <gui>Cancel</gui> to leave the product as
  it was.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lines">
  <title>Changing the order</title>
  <p>Right click a line to open its <gui>Product hub</gui>, move it up or
  down, or <gui>Move line to another PO</gui> of the same vendor, including a
  new one, when it was not on this invoice. <gui>Edit PO</gui> opens the order
  in the <link xref="purchase_order"/> window. <gui>Load items from
  file</gui> fills the order from a spreadsheet; see <link
  xref="purchase_order_import"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="correct">
  <title>Correcting an invoiced order</title>
  <p>To correct a bill already saved, open it from <link
  xref="vendor_payment"/>. The window opens as <gui>Correct Purchase
  Order</gui>: change the lines, right click a line to <gui>Cancel this
  line</gui>, and click <gui>Save corrections</gui>. The books are posted
  again to match. Only one person can correct an order at a time.</p>
  </section>

</page>
